---
description: Check Python dependencies for missing prebuilt wheels (ARM, Alpine/musl, Windows on ARM, free-threaded Python) with wheelgap, when a build compiles packages from source ("Failed building wheel for", "Microsoft Visual C++ 14.0 is required") or the user asks whether dependencies will install on a platform.
alwaysApply: false
---

- If the `wheelgap` MCP server is available, prefer its tools to the command line: `check_lockfile`, `check_dockerfile`, `check_dist` (a release's `dist/` folder, with `dropped`), `diff_lockfiles`, `check_package` and `list_targets`. From `check_lockfile` and `check_dockerfile`, read `ok` (true only when nothing has a gap) and `gaps` (the packages with one, with `results` and `error`); from `diff_lockfiles`, `worse`, `new` and `ok`; `check_lockfile`'s `passes` is the command line's verdict (it accepts gaps on advisory targets, and source builds with `fail-on = "missing"`); `check_package` takes `explain: true` for `rejected`, and `dependencies: true` to check everything it installs too (`dependency_gaps`, each with `via` and its own `suggest`/`last_with_wheel`; `ok` then covers the whole tree), and for a pinned version returns `suggest` and `last_with_wheel`; `check_lockfile` takes `dependencies: true` for a requirements file or `pyproject.toml`; statuses, `unknown` and `skipped` mean what they do below. Pass absolute paths or `file://` URIs. They take no `--no-dev`, `--group` or `--extra`, so use the command line for a selection `[tool.wheelgap]` doesn't configure. Setup: add a server running `uvx --from wheelgap wheelgap-mcp` to `.cursor/mcp.json` ([MCP setup guide](https://github.com/wheelgap/wheelgap/blob/main/docs/mcp.md)); if uvx says "An executable named `wheelgap-mcp` is not provided", run `uvx --refresh --from wheelgap wheelgap-mcp --version` once. `uvx wheelgap@latest mcp` starts the same server.
- Run `uvx wheelgap@latest -p <the project's Python> -t <platforms> --json` in the project directory, e.g. `-p 3.13 -t linux-aarch64,musl-aarch64`. Always pass `-p` (without it wheelgap may check free-threaded 3.14t). Or take both from a Dockerfile with `--dockerfile Dockerfile` (plus `--build-arg NAME=VALUE` for any value the build passes). If you don't know the platforms, `uvx wheelgap@latest --suggest-targets --json` reads them from the project's GitHub Actions workflows and Dockerfiles (checking nothing); confirm them with the user. `uvx wheelgap@latest --list-targets` lists targets.
- It finds `uv.lock`, `pylock.toml`, `poetry.lock`, `pdm.lock`, `Pipfile.lock`, `requirements.txt`, `requirements.lock` or `pixi.lock`; pass other files by path. For `pixi.lock`, it checks one environment's PyPI packages (`--environment NAME`) and skips conda ones. For a library with no lockfile, pass its `pyproject.toml` (direct dependencies only, unless you add `--deps`). To check whether `pip install NAME` works on a platform, pass the name with `--deps` (everything it installs, resolved per target from PyPI's metadata; `--explain` gives the path to each gap in `via`); pin names that are also subcommands, e.g. `mcp==2.3.0`. `--deps` resolves the whole tree of a requirements file or `pyproject.toml` the same way. To check exactly what pip resolved on a machine, pass the report from `python -m pip install --dry-run --ignore-installed --report report.json …` made there, with that machine's `-p` and `-t`. For a failed install, `--from-log install.log -p <Python> -t <platform>` finds the packages that failed to build and says why no wheel fits and which release has one.
- If the user names a deployment distro, pass `--distro NAME` (e.g. `ubuntu-22.04`, `alpine`; `--list-targets` lists them), or for one that isn't listed, verify its libc version and pass `--glibc X.Y` or `--musl X.Y`. None of these select platforms, so Alpine still needs a `musl-*` target.
- Read `summary.with_gaps` and each package's `results` (target label from `targets[].label` → `wheel`, `source`, `missing`, `unknown`, `n/a` or `bundled`), not just the exit code (`0`/`1` gaps/`2` bad input). `skipped` lists packages that weren't checked.
- Treat `unknown` as a failed lookup (see the package's `error`), not as fine. `--explain` adds why each wheel was rejected (`rejected`) and, for `uv.lock`, which dependency pulls it in (`via`), plus a link to the package's issue tracker when PyPI has one (`ask for a wheel: <url>`, `issues_url` in JSON); the text table names a transitive gap's direct dependency, e.g. `cryptography (via mcp)`; `--explain --notes` adds the coverage tracker's workarounds (`notes`), sending gap package names to wheelgap.github.io.
- Suggest fixes: pin a version that has the wheel (`--explain --suggest` names the nearest newer one, `suggest` in JSON, or the last older one when no newer one has it, `last_with_wheel`; on by default with `--deps --explain`, off with `--no-suggest`; `--write-constraints FILE` writes those pins as a pip/uv constraints file to check with `pip install -c FILE …`), use a `-slim` base instead of Alpine, or add build dependencies deliberately (`--explain --build-info` says what a source build needs; it downloads sdists). Accept a known gap with `--ignore NAME`, or `--ignore NAME:TARGET` for one platform (or `ignore = [...]` in `[tool.wheelgap]`), never by hiding it. To watch a platform without failing on it, add it to `-t` and pass `--advisory TARGET`.
- By default, `uv.lock`, `poetry.lock` and `pdm.lock` checks conservatively include extras and development groups. Match the deployment with `--no-dev`, `--group NAME` or `--only-group NAME` (and `--extra NAME` for uv.lock and Poetry 2.x poetry.lock) before treating a gap as a deployment failure.
- After a dependency upgrade, `uvx wheelgap@latest diff OLD NEW -p … -t … --json` lists what got worse; `OLD` can be a git ref such as `origin/main:uv.lock` (path relative to the repo root). `[tool.wheelgap]` doesn't apply to `diff`, so pass `-p` and `-t` (and `--distro` or `--glibc` for an older libc). The GitHub Action's `compare-to: base` runs this on pull requests.
- Before a release, `uvx wheelgap@latest --dist dist -p … -t …` checks the wheels and sdist about to be uploaded and fails when a target the previous PyPI release had a wheel for has none (`dropped` in JSON); the Action's `dist: dist` input does the same. `--badge PATH` writes a shields.io endpoint file for a README badge.
- For new-Python readiness, check `-p <current>,next` (or `next-t`): `blocking_newer_python` lists packages with a gap only on the newer one.
- wheelgap reports wheel availability only, not free-threading safety.
